      <h2 id="reaching-lhasa">Reaching Lhasa: Train vs. Flight</h2>
      <p>This is the single most important transport decision you will make for your Tibet trip. It is not just about time and money — it is about how hard the altitude hits you when you arrive.</p>

      <h3>Option A: The Qinghai-Tibet Railway — The Scenic, Safer Choice</h3>
      <p>The world's highest railway, reaching 5,072 meters at the Tanggula Pass. The train from Xining to Lhasa takes approximately 21 hours and climbs from 2,275m to 3,650m. Every car is pressurized and oxygen-enriched, and each compartment has individual oxygen outlets. The gradual ascent gives your body precious hours to begin acclimatizing while you watch the Tibetan Plateau unfold outside your window: yaks on the grasslands, snow-capped peaks, and the vast empty beauty of the Changtang.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Best departure city:</strong> Xining (2,275m). Spend a night here before boarding — it starts your acclimatization early.</li>
        <li><strong>Alternative departures:</strong> Chengdu (36 hours), Beijing (41 hours), Shanghai (47 hours). Longer journeys but the same gradual-acclimatization benefit.</li>
        <li><strong>Classes:</strong> Soft sleeper (4-berth compartment, door that locks, CNY 750–800) is the sweet spot. Hard sleeper (6-berth open compartment, CNY 500) is fine if you are on a budget. Hard seat for the full journey is not recommended.</li>
        <li><strong>Booking:</strong> Tickets open 15 days before departure and sell fast in peak season. Book through a travel agency or Trip.com (English interface). You need your passport to book and board.</li>
        <li><strong>Onboard:</strong> Dining car serves Chinese meals. Bring your own snacks and a large water bottle. Toilets are basic (squat style). Charging outlets are available in soft sleeper compartments.</li>
        <li><strong>The scenery:</strong> The best views are between Golmud and Lhasa (roughly hours 10–20). You pass through the Kunlun Mountains, the Hoh Xil nature reserve (look for Tibetan antelope), and the Tanggula Pass before descending into the Lhasa Valley.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Option B: Flying — Faster, But Harder on Your Body</h3>
      <p>Direct flights operate from approximately 15 Chinese cities. The flight itself is easy. The challenge is what happens when you land: your body has gone from sea level (or moderate altitude) to 3,650 meters in two hours with zero acclimatization time.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Direct flights from:</strong> Chengdu (2h, the most popular route), Chongqing (2.5h), Xi'an (3h), Beijing (4h), Shanghai (5h), Kunming (2.5h), and several others.</li>
        <li><strong>Price range:</strong> CNY 800–2,000 one-way, depending on season and booking lead time.</li>
        <li><strong>Mitigation strategy:</strong> If flying, spend 2–3 days in Chengdu (500m) or Kunming (1,900m) first. This partial acclimatization makes a measurable difference. Even one night at moderate altitude is better than flying directly from sea level.</li>
        <li><strong>Lhasa Gonggar Airport (LXA):</strong> Located 60 km southwest of the city in the Yarlung Tsangpo River valley. The airport is modern but small. Baggage claim is efficient. The thin air is noticeable the moment you step outside.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="airport-transfer">Airport to City Center</h2>
      <p>The 60 km journey from Lhasa Gonggar Airport to the city takes about an hour. Three options:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Airport shuttle bus (CNY 30):</strong> The most economical option. Buses depart when full (usually every 30–60 minutes) from directly outside the arrivals terminal. The destination is the Civil Aviation Bureau (民航局) terminal on Niangre Road, approximately 1 km north of Potala Palace. From there, a short taxi (CNY 10) or 15-minute walk gets you to the palace area. The bus stops at the Lhasa Railway Station en route if passengers need it.</li>
        <li><strong>Taxi (CNY 200–250):</strong> Available outside arrivals. Agree on the price before getting in (meters are rarely used for airport runs). 60 minutes. Have your hotel address written in Chinese on your phone.</li>
        <li><strong>Hotel pickup (CNY 300–400):</strong> Arrange through your hotel when booking. Slightly more expensive than a taxi but worthwhile for: (a) a driver holding a sign with your name, (b) guaranteed vehicle quality, and (c) less stress after a flight when you are already dealing with altitude.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="train-station">Lhasa Railway Station</h2>
      <p>Located on the south bank of the Lhasa River, approximately 8 km southwest of Potala Palace. The station is large, modern, and well-organized. Taxi to the city center costs CNY 30–40 (15–20 minutes). Buses 1, 13, and 14 connect the station to the city center (CNY 1–2), but navigating them with luggage at altitude is not recommended for first-time visitors.</p>

      <h2 id="getting-around">Getting Around Lhasa</h2>

      <h3>Taxis</h3>
      <p>The workhorses of Lhasa transport. Plentiful, metered, and cheap. Base fare: CNY 10 for the first 3 km, then CNY 2 per additional km. A ride from Barkhor to Potala is CNY 10–15. Most drivers do not speak English — have your destination written in Chinese characters or show them on a map. Taxis are generally honest about using meters within the city. After 10 PM, fares increase slightly.</p>

      <h3>Public Buses</h3>
      <p>CNY 1–2 per ride. Extensive network covering the entire city. The most useful lines for visitors: Bus 8 (Barkhor to Potala), Bus 13 (Railway Station to city center), Bus 24 (Sera Monastery). Buses can be crowded and are entirely in Chinese. They are an experience, not a convenience choice — but at CNY 1, they are the cheapest form of transport in any Chinese city.</p>

      <h3>Walking</h3>
      <p>Lhasa's central attractions are walkable. Potala Palace to Jokhang Temple: 2 km, 25 minutes. Jokhang to Barkhor: the Barkhor is the circumambulation path around Jokhang — they are physically connected. The walk is flat, paved, and fascinating. However, at 3,650 meters, walking distances that would be trivial at sea level become genuine efforts. Plan for rest stops and double your normal walking time estimates.</p>

      <h3>Bicycle and E-Bike Rental</h3>
      <p>Shared bikes (Meituan, Hellobike) are available in Lhasa. You need a Chinese payment app to unlock them. E-bikes are ideal — the electric assist negates the altitude's effect on your stamina. Rental shops near Barkhor offer half-day and full-day bicycle rentals (CNY 50–100/day). Cycling the Barkhor circuit on a bike is not permitted — it is a pedestrian-only zone.</p>

      <h2 id="reaching-potala">Getting to Potala Palace</h2>
      <p>Potala Palace sits on the Red Hill in the exact center of Lhasa. It is visible from almost everywhere in the city, so you cannot get lost — just look up.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>By taxi:</strong> Tell the driver "布达拉宫" (Bùdálā Gōng). They will drop you at the east gate or the base of the palace approach road. CNY 10–15 from most central locations.</li>
        <li><strong>On foot from Barkhor:</strong> Walk west along Beijing East Road for approximately 20–25 minutes. The palace grows larger with every step. Cross the large intersection in front of the palace and approach from the south side for the full dramatic reveal.</li>
        <li><strong>The entrance:</strong> Visitor entry is through the east gate (the uphill approach with the stone steps). The south gate faces the square and is exit-only. Do not walk to the south gate expecting to enter.</li>
        <li><strong>After your visit:</strong> You exit through the west gate. From there, a path leads down to Beijing Middle Road. Taxis are available on this road. The walk to Barkhor from the west gate is approximately 15 minutes downhill.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="day-trips">Day Trips and Beyond Lhasa</h2>
      <p>Most visitors to Lhasa also want to see Tibet beyond the city. Here is a quick overview — more detail is in the full Tibet transport pages (coming soon).</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Ganden Monastery:</strong> 45 km east. Minibus from the Ganden bus station near Barkhor (CNY 30, 1.5 hours). Departs at 6:30–7:00 AM, returns around 2:00 PM. Arrange a taxi for the day (CNY 400–500 round trip) for more flexibility.</li>
        <li><strong>Drepung Monastery:</strong> 8 km west. Bus 24 or taxi (CNY 30). A half-day trip.</li>
        <li><strong>Yamdrok Lake:</strong> 100 km southwest. Tour vehicles only (CNY 300–500 per person for a group day tour). The road crosses Kamba-la Pass (4,794m) with spectacular lake views. Requires an Aliens' Travel Permit in addition to your TTP.</li>
        <li><strong>Nam Tso Lake:</strong> 220 km north. Full-day trip (CNY 400–600). One of Tibet's three sacred lakes at 4,718 meters. The altitude is significantly higher than Lhasa — do not attempt on your first or second day.</li>
        <li><strong>Shigatse and Everest Base Camp:</strong> Multi-day tours from Lhasa. Shigatse is 270 km (5–6 hours by road). EBC is an additional 340 km beyond Shigatse. Minimum 4–5 days round trip. These are major undertakings that need dedicated planning beyond the scope of this guide.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="practical">Practical Transport Tips at Altitude</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Arrive with time to acclimatize.</strong> Do not schedule any transport-heavy activities for your first 24 hours in Lhasa. Your body is working hard just breathing. Do not make it also navigate a new city.</li>
        <li><strong>Carry water everywhere.</strong> The dry air at altitude dehydrates you faster than you realize. Have at least 1 liter of water with you on any transport leg longer than 30 minutes.</li>
        <li><strong>Sun protection in vehicles.</strong> The UV index at Lhasa's altitude can exceed 12 in summer. Car windows do not block UVA rays. Wear sunscreen even for a 10-minute taxi ride.</li>
        <li><strong>Road conditions:</strong> Roads within Lhasa are excellent. Outside the city, highways are generally good but can be affected by landslides in summer (July–August monsoon) and ice in winter (December–February). Mountain passes can close temporarily after heavy snow.</li>
        <li><strong>Altitude and vehicles:</strong> If you are prone to carsickness, high-altitude winding roads will amplify it. Bring motion sickness medication if doing any day trips into the mountains.</li>
        <li><strong>Fuel stations:</strong> In Lhasa, plentiful. On long-distance routes outside the city, fill up whenever you see a station. Distances between stations can exceed 100 km in remote areas.</li>
      </ul>
